Microsoft Learn: Configure Azure Storage Accounts

Platform: Microsoft Learn
Module: Configure Azure Storage Accounts
Track: AZ-104 Microsoft Azure Administrator
Difficulty: Intermediate
Date: 2026

Objective

Learn how to configure and manage Azure Storage Accounts, including selecting storage types, implementing replication strategies, and securing storage access for enterprise cloud environments.

This module focused on the core storage services used in Microsoft Azure and how Azure Administrators deploy scalable, reliable, and secure storage solutions for modern applications.

Step 1: Understanding Azure Storage Accounts

The module introduced Azure Storage Accounts as the foundation of Azure cloud storage services.

Azure Storage Accounts provide scalable cloud storage for:

Blobs (object storage)
Files (managed file shares)
Queues (message storage)
Tables (NoSQL structured data)

Key benefits:

High availability
Scalability
Durability
Secure access management
Integration with Azure services

A storage account acts as a container that groups Azure storage services together under one management configuration.

Step 2: Storage Account Types

The module covered different Azure Storage Account types and their use cases.

Main storage account types:
Storage Type	Purpose
General-purpose v2 (GPv2)	Recommended default account type supporting all services
Blob Storage	Optimized for blob/object storage
FileStorage	Premium file shares
BlockBlobStorage	Premium performance for block blobs
Key concepts learned:
GPv2 accounts provide the widest compatibility and features
Premium storage is optimized for high-performance workloads
Standard storage is cost-effective for general usage
Choosing the correct storage type impacts performance and pricing
Step 3: Azure Storage Replication Strategies

A major focus of the module was redundancy and replication.

Azure automatically replicates data to protect against hardware failures and outages.

Replication options studied:
Replication Type	Description
LRS	Locally Redundant Storage
ZRS	Zone-Redundant Storage
GRS	Geo-Redundant Storage
RA-GRS	Read-Access Geo-Redundant Storage
GZRS	Geo-Zone-Redundant Storage
Important takeaways:
LRS stores multiple copies within one datacenter
ZRS replicates across availability zones
GRS replicates data to a secondary geographic region
RA-GRS allows read access to replicated secondary data
Replication strategy selection depends on cost, availability, and disaster recovery requirements

The module emphasized balancing business continuity requirements with operational costs.

Step 4: Configuring Secure Access

The training also covered security configurations for storage accounts.

Security features explored:
Storage account firewalls
Virtual network restrictions
Private endpoints
Shared Access Signatures (SAS)
Access keys
Azure role-based access control (RBAC)
Key lessons:
Public access should be restricted whenever possible
RBAC is preferred over sharing account keys
Private endpoints increase security by removing public internet exposure
SAS tokens provide temporary delegated access to storage resources

Security is a critical responsibility for Azure Administrators managing enterprise environments.

Step 5: Real-World Scenario

The module used an example of a large e-commerce company storing product images in Azure Storage.

Key administrative goals included:

Supporting high traffic loads
Ensuring high availability
Protecting data against outages
Enabling rapid disaster recovery
Maintaining secure access controls

This demonstrated how Azure Storage Accounts support scalable enterprise cloud architectures.

